Independent reviews of the pool core's theorem statements against their docstrings, and of the
shell's reuse logic, on 2026-10-29.

claude-prompt-1.txt, claude-review-1.txt
    A fresh Claude Opus subagent (no context from the session), reading a snapshot of the tree
    (git archive of the branch at a5de80f, before the history was tidied).
gemini-prompt-1.txt, gemini-review-1.txt
    Gemini through ~/.local/bin/agy-readonly (model gemini-4-argon-medium, read-only sandbox), run
    from the worktree, which changed while it read (the fixes for the Claude review went in during
    its run); its line numbers are those of the tree it saw.

What came of each finding (C = Claude, G = Gemini):

Fixed in the shell (LeanHttp/Client.lean, LeanHttp/Config.lean):
- C1  Client.close did nothing with pooling off: Call.attempt now checks the pool's closed flag
      with pooling off too (net scenario pool-close-off).  Config says maxActivePerOrigin applies
      only with pooling on.
- C2, G4  After a re-dispatch, an error whose second attempt wrote nothing said "not sent" though
      the first attempt may have been delivered: it now says maybe delivered, unless the
      re-dispatch followed a 408 (net scenario redispatch-refused).
- C3, G1  Exception paths: the exchange's catch now cancels any pending receive, idleClean cancels
      its wait even if the timer throws, and the wait at the active cap is wrapped so that request
      does not throw.
- C4, G2  The idle check is bounded by the time left, acquire checks the deadline first, connect
      starts no name lookup once the deadline has passed (a call sent again could get there late),
      and an exchange that timed out before writing anything gives its connection back instead of
      dropping a healthy one.
- C7  maxActivePerOrigin = some 0 is refused as an invalid request (unit check); idleTimeoutMs = 0
      turning reuse off is documented.
- G3  The time passed to the pool is read under the mutex, so the pool sees times in the order of
      its operations.
- C9  PoolState.freed's docstring says close resolves it too; DESIGN.md no longer says a dropped
      connection is shut down for writing.

Fixed in the proofs and docstrings (LeanHttp/Pool.lean, LeanHttp/Pool/Proofs.lean):
- C10, G6  "Returned or dropped exactly once" now reads "once per checkout", says at most one
      return per hand-out (exactly one if the shell ends every call), and that the pool knows
      connections by id, not by the call holding them: the per-call half is the shell's.
- C11, G10  New theorems sweep_letGo and letGo_gone: every connection an operation lets go (for the
      shell to close) was idle and is gone afterwards.
- C12, G7  New theorem drop_not_out; giveBack_not_out's docstring no longer claims drop.
- C13, G9  Pool.lean's module doc and Returned.notOut say that a give-back of a connection not
      checked out still lets the expired idle connections go.
- C14, G8  New theorems take_fresh_unused (under the invariant, a new connection's id was never
      allocated), take_closed, apply_closed and run_closed (once closed, a pool stays closed and
      hands nothing out); the module doc cites state_gone, and says the preference for the most
      recently returned connection is not proved (pool-tests checks it).
- G10  giveBack_unreusable_gone and drop_gone's docstrings say "checked-out".

Recorded, not changed:
- C5  The NOTES.md section on the pool, with the idle-check measurements, was not yet written when
      the snapshot was taken; it is now.  DESIGN.md's out-of-date lines were fixed in the same way.
      The libuv loop order the 0 ms window relies on was checked against libuv 1.48's source and
      design document (UV_RUN_ONCE polls before it runs due timers) and against Lean's event loop
      (every registration interrupts uv_run, which the loop thread then re-enters).
- C6, G5  The Redispatch.notSent arm cannot fire with the current exchange core, which counts a
      request as possibly delivered from its first write; it stays as a guard, and its docstring
      says so.
- C8  The wake-up at the active cap is shared by all origins and serves waiters in no order;
      documented in Config and NOTES.md.

Second round:

gemini-prompt-2.txt, gemini-review-2.txt
    Gemini again, told what came of the first round, on the final tree (16e5c51, before these logs
    were committed).  It found the fixes sound and every path still one give-back or drop per
    connection, and made two observations, neither changed:
- A write still in flight when an exception ends the exchange keeps its socket until the write
  ends: the known limit of v4.34.1 (no way to cancel a write), already in NOTES.md.
- That giveBack's let-go list never holds the connection given back follows from letGo_gone and
  WF.state_out, but is not a theorem of its own.
